      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment of an OIML certificate.\n\nDocument-level info:\n- Number: R49/2013-A-NL1-21.03 revision 6\n- Scheme: A (stated \"issued under scheme A\")\n- Project number: 3891220\n- Page total: 7\n- Member state: The Netherlands\n- Date issued: 31 July 2025 (from \"NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1, 31 July 2025\")\n- OIML issuer ID: NL1\n\nIssuing authority:\n- Name: NMi Certin B.V. (OIML Issuing Authority NL1)\n- Address lines: Thijsseweg 11, 2629 JA Delft, The Netherlands\n- Person responsible: M.Ph.D. Schmidt\n- Phone: +31 88 636 2332\n- Email: certin@nmi.nl\n- Website: www.nmi.nl\n- oiml_issuer_id: NL1\n\nApplicants:\n- Badger Meter, Inc., 4545 West Brown Deer Road, Milwaukee, WI 53224, United States of America\n\nManufacturers:\n- Badger Meter Europe GmbH, Nürtinger Straße 76, 72639 Neuffen, Germany\n\nCertified type:\n- Category: \"Water meter\" (the bold in \"An electromagnetic water meter\"). Category examples given include \"Water meter\" isn't listed but fits. Let's use \"Water meter\".\n- type_designations: [\"M5000\"]\n- module_designation: The type is \"An electromagnetic water meter\" — hmm, module_designation is for modules like \"Analog load cell\". Here it's a water meter, not a module. Probably null or omit. The description: \"An electromagnetic water meter\". I could put description: \"Electromagnetic water meter\". Let me include description \"An electromagnetic water meter\".\n\nRecommendation:\n- R 49-1 (2013) → id: \"R49-1\", edition: 2013? The schema says edition (int). For R49-1, the edition year 2013. Hmm, edition is usually like \"edition 2013\". Set edition: 2013. Accuracy classes: [\"1\", \"2\"].\n\nTest reports:\n- NMi-13200198-01 Revision 1, dated 7 July 2022, 29 pages\n- NMi-3531004-01 revision 1, dated 4 July 2023, 20 pages\n- NMi-3891220-01, dated 17 April 2025, 19 pages\n- NMi-3891220-02, dated 31 July 2025, 18 pages\n\nRole: these are type evaluation reports? The text says \"conformity was established by the results of tests and examinations provided in the associated reports\". The certificate mentions \"OIML Type Evaluation Report\". I'll set role: \"type_evaluation_report\" for these. Actually the schema offers roles: type_evaluation_report | test_report | documentation_file | evaluation_report. These associated reports — likely type evaluation report. I'll use \"type_evaluation_report\".\n\nDocumentation folder: \"T10554-4\" — \"The construction of the measuring instrument is recorded in the Documentation folder no. T10554-4.\" That's a documentation_file test report entry: id \"T10554-4\".\n\nRevision history: revision \"6\" — date? The revision 6 date is 31 July 2025 presumably (issue date). Changes unknown. Should I include revision_history with revision \"6\", date 2025-07-31, changes null? The rules say don't fabricate. The revision is mentioned in the number. Date of issue is 31 July 2025. I could include revision_history: [{\"revision\": \"6\", \"date\": \"2025-07-31\"}]. Hmm, it's plausible but the date is the certificate issue date. I'll include it cautiously — maybe omit changes. Actually, maybe safer to include since revision 6 is clearly current and date issued is 31 July 2025.\n\nCharacteristics (Table 1 General characteristics) — type_level:\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic flow metering\" (string value)\n- accuracy_class: \"1 and 2\" → could be a list? Schema says value can be number|minmax|string. Accuracy class 1 and 2. I'll represent as string \"1 and 2\" or maybe list. Keep string \"1 and 2\". Or better: value \"1, 2\"? Hmm. I'll use \"1 and 2\". Actually maybe accuracy classes belongs to recommendation.accuracy_classes: [\"1\",\"2\"]. For characteristics, string \"1 and 2\".\n- environmental_class: \"M1 / O (installed outdoors)\" → string. Maybe value \"M1 / O\" with note. Keep as string \"M1 / O (installed outdoors)\". Hmm, the value is \"M1 / O\" with clarification. I'll keep full string.\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E2\"\n- temperature_range_ambient: -25 °C / +55 °C → {min: -25, max: 55}, unit °C\n- water_temperature_class: T50 (+0,1 °C / +50 °C) → value string? T50 class means water temp range 0.1 to 50 °C. Could do value \"T50\" plus range. I'll make water_temperature_class: {value: \"T50\", ...} and maybe a separate characteristic water_temperature_range: {min: 0.1, max: 50} °C. That's reasonable extraction. Or keep single string. I'll do water_temperature_class: {value: \"T50\"} and water_temperature_range: {value: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}.\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1,6 MPa (16 bar) → value 1.6, unit \"MPa\"; add secondary? Could add maximum_admissible_pressure_bar: 16 bar. I'll include map 1.6 MPa and maybe note. Keep one characteristic with value 1.6 unit MPa; maybe include \"(16 bar)\" as separate characteristic maximum_admissible_pressure_bar value 16 unit bar. I'll include both for fidelity? That's a bit redundant but fine. Actually simpler: value 1.6, unit_symbol \"MPa\". The parenthetical is a conversion. I'll add second one too — harmless. Hmm, rule: don't fabricate; conversion is present. I'll include \"maximum_admissible_pressure_alt\" no... I'll just do the MPa one and mention bar in... The structured value doesn't have notes. I'll include both characteristics: maximum_admissible_pressure (1.6 MPa) and maximum_admissible_pressure_bar? That label is odd. Maybe skip the bar. I'll skip it. Actually, I could keep it as one value string? No — strict structured values. Keep 1.6 MPa only.\n\nHmm, but maybe better to preserve. I'll add `maximum_admissible_pressure_equivalent`? Too noisy. Skip.\n\n- orientation: \"All positions (Horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\" → string\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\" → string\n- reverse_flow: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\" → string / true? Keep string.\n- pressure_loss_class: two values by bore configuration: \"Full bore meter Δp 10 (0,10 bar)\", \"Reduce bore Δp 40 (0,40 bar)\". This varies by configuration — full bore vs reduced bore. This is a config_level entry! attribute: pressure_loss_class, axis: bore_type, values: [{condition: \"full bore\", value: \"Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\"}, {condition: \"reduced bore\", value: \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\"}]. Structured: value could be \"Δp 10\" with unit... The class is Δp 10 which corresponds to 0.10 bar. I'd do value: \"Δp10 (0.10 bar)\"? Better structured: value \"Δp 10\", unit null; plus pressure loss value 0.10 bar. Let me do config_level with values {condition: \"Full bore meter\", value: \"Δp 10\", ...} hmm but numeric 0.10 bar available. Alternative: attribute \"pressure_loss\", axis \"bore\", values [{condition \"full bore\", value 0.1, unit \"bar\"}, {condition \"reduced bore\", value 0.4, unit \"bar\"}]. And pressure_loss_class separately: {value: \"Δp 10 / Δp 40\"}? Hmm.\n\nI think config_level: attribute \"pressure_loss_class\", axis \"bore_type\", values [{condition \"Full bore meter\", value \"Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\"}, {condition \"Reduced bore\", value \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\"}]. But strict value objects need value/unit split. Could do value: \"Δp 10\", unit null, plus separate attribute pressure_loss config-level with numeric. I'll do two config_level entries:\n1. attribute: pressure_loss_class, axis: bore_type, values: [{condition: \"Full bore meter\", value: \"Δp 10\"}, {condition: \"Reduced bore\", value: \"Δp 40\"}]\n2. attribute: maximum_pressure_loss, axis: bore_type, values: [{condition: \"Full bore meter\", value: 0.1, unit \"bar\"}, {condition: \"Reduced bore\", value: 0.4, unit \"bar\"}]\n\nNote the config_level value shape per schema: \"values[]: {condition, value}\" — rule 5 says \"Values follow the same structured form: {condition, value, unit_symbol}\". OK so include unit_symbol.\n\n- power_supply: \"Replaceable battery (2,9 – 3,7 V)\" → power_supply_type: \"Replaceable battery\"; power_supply_voltage: {min: 2.9, max: 3.7}, unit \"V\".\n- software_identification: \"See table 5\" → omit? It's a characteristic but value is a reference. Could record software_identification: {value: \"See table 5\"}. Not present in this fragment (table 5 presumably later). I might include it as string or omit. I'll include as string \"See table 5\"? Hmm, rule 8: empty strings not values. \"See table 5\" is a value of sorts. I'll include it — harmless. Actually maybe omit since it's a pointer. I'll include; it indicates software varies, described in table 5 which isn't in this fragment.\n\nTables 2 and 3: family of instruments with accuracy class 1 (Table 2) and class 2 (Table 3), each with meter sizes DN50..DN600, flow rates Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4, ratio Q3/Q1.\n\nThis is a model family: models keyed by meter size (DN50, DN65, ..., DN600) plus reduced bore variants (class 2 only: DN50 RB, DN65 RB, DN80 RB, DN100 RB). And attributes vary by accuracy class too — so it's a 2-D structure: model × accuracy class.\n\nHow to model? Options:\n- model_id includes accuracy class: e.g. \"DN50 (class 1)\", \"DN50 (class 2)\", \"DN50 RB (class 2)\". That's clean but many models (14 + 18 = 32 models).\n- Or emit two matrix_tables (Table 2 and Table 3) and model_level entries with values per model, where model_id distinguishes class.\n\nGiven the R137 example, I should extract matrix_tables[] and populate model_family.models and characteristics.model_level.\n\nModel list: class 1 sizes: DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600 (14). Class 2: those 14 plus 4 reduced bore (DN50 RB, DN65 RB, DN80 RB, DN100 RB) = 18.\n\nI'll create model_ids like \"DN50-class1\", \"DN65-class1\", ... and \"DN50-class2\", \"DN50-RB-class2\"... Hmm. Alternatively, keep model_id as size string as in tables (\"DN50 reduced bore*\") and have separate model_level attribute entries per class: e.g. attribute \"q1\", with two entries? The model_level schema: attribute, unit, values[{model, value}]. For class dimension, I could name models \"DN50 (class 1)\" and \"DN50 (class 2)\". That keeps unique model keys.\n\nLet me use model_id strings exactly as in tables but disambiguated: \"DN50 (class 1)\" ... \"DN600 (class 1)\", \"DN50 (class 2)\", \"DN50 RB (class 2)\", ..., \"DN600 (class 2)\".\n\nThe footnote * on reduced bore: \"* For the reduced bore (RB) version, the size mentioned is the process connection size. On the inscriptions of the water meter the reduced bore meter will be identified as 'DNxxx RB'.\" → footnotes: marker \"*\", text. And footnote_markers on those model values? Model IDs aren't structured values with footnote_markers in schema, but matrix table rows could have footnotes. I'll add footnotes[] entry with marker \"*\".\n\nmatrix_tables:\n1. name \"General characteristics of the family of instruments with accuracy class 1\", columns: [\"meter_size\", \"diameter_inlet_outlet\", \"q1_minimum\", \"q2_transitional\", \"q3_permanent\", \"q4_overload\", \"ratio_q3_q1\"], column_units: [null, \"mm\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null], rows: 14 rows with numbers (decimal conversion: 0,315 → 0.315; 78,75 → 78.75; 312,5 → 312.5; 15,625 → 15.625; 39,375 → 39.375; 50,400 → 50.4; 80,640 → 80.64).\n\nDN600 class1: Q1 50,400 → 50.4; Q2 80,640 → 80.64; Q3 6300; Q4 7875; ratio 125.\n\n2. name \"General characteristics of the family of instruments with accuracy class 2\", same columns, rows 18 including reduced bore rows with footnote marker \"*\". Rows as objects; I can add a \"footnote_markers\" field per row? The schema says rows[] (object per row). I'll include footnote_markers: [\"*\"] on the reduced bore rows. That seems acceptable.\n\nmodel_level characteristics: Should I emit q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio per model? That would be 5 attributes × 32 models. That's a lot of JSON but it's the \"MOST IMPORTANT\" rule. Let me emit them: attributes q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1, diameter_inlet_outlet (also varies). Diameter per model: DN50→50 etc. That's attribute 6.\n\nHmm, 32 models × 6 attributes... large but fine. Let me consider: is it better to keep models per class? Maybe reduce redundancy: emit model_level entries with model ids including class. Yes.\n\nActually, maybe I can simplify: since table rows fully capture it, and model_level is required by rule 4 (\"EXTRACT as matrix_tables[] AND populate model_family.models and characteristics.model_level\"), I'll do it.\n\nLet me define models:\n\nClass 1: DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600 → model_id \"DN50 (class 1)\" etc.\n\nClass 2: DN50 (class 2), DN50 RB (class 2), DN65 (class 2), DN65 RB (class 2), DN80 (class 2), DN80 RB (class 2), DN100 (class 2), DN100 RB (class 2), DN125 (class 2), DN150 (class 2), DN200 (class 2), DN250 (class 2), DN300 (class 2), DN350 (class 2), DN400 (class 2), DN450 (class 2), DN500 (class 2), DN600 (class 2).\n\nTotal 32 models.\n\nmodel_level attributes:\n- diameter_inlet_outlet (mm)\n- q1 (m³/h) minimum flow\n- q2 (m³/h) transitional\n- q3 (m³/h) permanent\n- q4 (m³/h) overload\n- ratio_q3_q1 (dimensionless)\n\nLet me compile values.\n\nClass 1 (Table 2):\n- DN50: Ø50, Q1 0.315, Q2 0.504, Q3 63, Q4 78.75, ratio 200\n- DN65: 65, 0.5, 0.8, 100, 125, 200\n- DN80: 80, 0.8, 1.28, 160, 200, 200\n- DN100: 100, 1, 1.6, 250, 312.5, 250\n- DN125: 125, 1.6, 2.56, 400, 500, 250\n- DN150: 150, 3.9375, 6.3, 630, 787.5, 160\n- DN200: 200, 6.25, 10, 1000, 1250, 160\n- DN250: 250, 10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160\n- DN300: 300, 15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, 160\n- DN350: 350, 15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, 160\n- DN400: 400, 25, 40, 4000, 5000, 160\n- DN450: 450, 39.375, 63, 6300, 7875, 160\n- DN500: 500, 39.375, 63, 6300, 7875, 160\n- DN600: 600, 50.4, 80.64, 6300, 7875, 125\n\nClass 2 (Table 3):\n- DN50: 50, 0.315, 0.504, 63, 78.75, 200\n- DN50 RB: 50, 0.158, 0.25, 63, 78.75, 400\n- DN65: 65, 0.5, 0.8, 100, 125, 200\n- DN65 RB: 65, 0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400\n- DN80: 80, 0.8, 1.28, 160, 200, 200\n- DN80 RB: 80, 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400\n- DN100: 100, 1, 1.6, 250, 312.5, 250\n- DN100 RB: 100, 0.63, 1.00, 250, 312.5, 400\n- DN125: 125, 1.6, 2.56, 400, 500, 250\n- DN150: 150, 2.52, 4.032, 630, 787.5, 250\n- DN200: 200, 6.4, 10.24, 1600, 2000, 250\n- DN250: 250, 6.4, 10.24, 1600, 2000, 250\n- DN300: 300, 10, 16, 2500, 3125, 250\n- DN350: 350, 10, 16, 2500, 3125, 250\n- DN400: 400, 16, 25.6, 4000, 5000, 250\n- DN450: 450, 25.2, 40.32, 6300, 7875, 250\n- DN500: 500, 25.2, 40.32, 6300, 7875, 250\n- DN600: 600, 40, 64, 10000, 12500, 250\n\nNote DN250 class 2: Q1 6.4 same as DN200 — as given, keep.\n\nNow write JSON. This will be long but fine.\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"M5000\"? The family of instruments... The type is M5000, and tables describe \"the family of instruments\". family_name could be \"M5000\". Rule: \"explicit family name if any, else null\". The family isn't explicitly named beyond type M5000. I'd set family_name \"M5000\"? Hmm — the certified type M5000 covers the family. I think family_name: \"M5000\" is reasonable but might be considered fabrication. The doc says \"Tables 2 – 4 give an overview of the general characteristics of the family of instruments.\" No explicit family name. I'll set family_name: null and models list. Actually the models ARE variants of M5000. I'll set \"M5000\" — it is the type designation covering the family, and it aids identification. Hmm... I'll go with \"M5000\" since the family is the M5000 family. Risk of \"fabrication\" low.\n\ncertificate:\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-21.03\"\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- project_number: \"3891220\"\n- page_total: 7\n- member_state: \"The Netherlands\"\n- date_issued: \"2025-07-31\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\nrevision: \"6\" — where? Schema doesn't show revision in certificate fields... The number includes \"revision 6\". revision_history: [{revision: \"6\", date: \"2025-07-31\", changes: null}]. I'll include with changes omitted or null. Schema shows changes field; I'll set changes: null? Rule 8 says omit if 